## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a small, practical Azure Functions workflow with per-tenant isolation, blob bindings, input validation, and a maintenance job. It also checks testing strategy for bindings.

## Key Concepts
- HTTP Trigger and BlobOutput bindings
- Path structuring for multi-tenant isolation
- Input validation and size enforcement
- Idempotent upload semantics
- TimerTrigger for background cleanup
- Basic testing using unit tests and local emulators

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example Azure Function HTTP trigger with BlobOutput binding (Node.js)
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenantId = req.params.tenantId;
  const { fileName, contentBase64 } = req.body || {};

  // Validations
  if (!tenantId || !/^tenant-[a-z0-9-]+$/.test(tenantId)) {
    context.res = { status: 400, body: 'Invalid tenantId' };
    return;
  }
  if (!fileName || !contentBase64) {
    context.res = { status: 400, body: 'Missing fields' };
    return;
  }

  const buffer = Buffer.from(contentBase64, 'base64');
  if (buffer.length > 1024 * 1024) {
    context.res = { status: 413, body: 'File too large' };
    return;
  }

  // Blob path uses tenant-specific prefix
  const blobPath = `tenant-${tenantId}/${fileName}`;
  // Binding setup (depends on function.json):
  context.bindings.blobOut = { data: buffer, blobPath };

  context.res = { status: 200, body: 'Uploaded' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add API key or AAD-based authentication for this endpoint?
- How would you extend to streaming uploads for larger files without loading all data into memory?
- How would you monitor and test this end-to-end in a CI/CD pipeline?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess practical Azure devops, security, and data isolation in a beginner-friendly stack. The candidate must describe concrete steps, not abstractions.

## Key Concepts
- Function Apps, HTTP triggers, and binding to Cosmos DB
- Partition keys and per-tenant isolation in Cosmos DB SQL API
- Secrets management with Azure Key Vault and managed identities
- Per-environment configuration with Azure App Configuration
- GitHub Actions multi-environment deployment
- Testing: unit + integration (Cosmos DB Emulator)
- Cost control: Consumption plan, autoscale, budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// index.js (Azure Function)
const { CosmosClient } = require('@azure/cosmos');
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenantId = (req.headers['x-tenant-id'] || req.query.tenantId);
  const client = new CosmosClient(process.env.COSMOS_CONNECTION_STRING);
  const container = client.database('TenantDB').container('Notes');
  const item = { id: req.body.id || require('uuid').v4(), tenantId, note: req.body.note };
  const { resource } = await container.items.create(item);
  context.res = { status: 201, body: resource };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data isolation across tenants in CI?
- Which Cosmos DB consistency level would you choose and why in a 2-region setup?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluate ability to implement observability across heterogeneous runtimes in Azure, with end-to-end tracing and cross-service propagation.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry across Functions, AKS, Databricks
- W3C TraceContext; OTLP exporting to Azure Monitor
- Context propagation through queues and Delta Lake
- Sampling strategies and trace retention

## Code Example
```javascript
const { trace, context, propagation } = require('@opentelemetry/api');
const { NodeTracerProvider } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node');
const { SimpleSpanProcessor } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-base');
const { OTLPTraceExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-otlp-http');

const provider = new NodeTracerProvider();
const exporter = new OTLPTraceExporter({ url: 'https://<region>.monitor.azure.com/v1/traces' });
provider.addSpanProcessor(new SimpleSpanProcessor(exporter));
provider.register();

module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tracer = trace.getTracer('example-function');
  const span = tracer.startSpan('http-request');
  // downstream calls propagate context automatically when using OpenTelemetry API
  span.end();
  return { status: 200, body: 'OK' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test end-to-end traces across services?
- How would you adjust sampling in production without outages?

## Why This Is Asked
